Article 8
(1) In the effort to protect women and children from acts of violence, the obligation is to:

a. guarantee the rights of women and children without discrimination in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;

b. establish and implement policy programs and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children;

c. facilitate the formation of community organizations concerned with women and children;

d. conduct cooperation with the community and/or community organizations and the private sector in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

e. conduct rehabilitation and empowerment of women and children who are victims of acts of violence;

f. provide special protection to women and children victims of various acts of violence; and

g. take preventive action in order to prevent violations of the rights of women and children.

(2) The responsibility in the effort to protect women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by regional apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

(3) Further provisions regarding the obligations of the City Government as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by the Mayor's Regulation.

Article 14
(1) The implementation of handling of women and children victims of acts of violence includes:
a. handling of complaints from victims of violence;
b. health services; and
c. law enforcement and legal assistance.
(2) The implementation of handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through integrated services.
(3) Integrated services as referred to in paragraph (2) for women and children victims of acts of violence are conducted with the principles of:
a. responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relations;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims;
j. using language that is appropriate and understood by victims;
k. fast and simple;
l. empathy;
m. fulfillment of the rights of women and children; and
n. legal certainty.

Article 15
The handling of women and child victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (1) letter a is carried out by:
a. hospitals;
b. public health centers;
c. Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children;
d. Safe houses;
e. Women Crisis Centre;
f. Agency for Marriage Counseling, Development and Preservation;
g. Police;
h. Prosecutor's Office;
i. Courts;
j. Ministry of Law and Human Rights;
k. Legal Aid Institute; and
l. Other Institutions.

Article 21
(1) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 20 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:
a. assist victims in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them independently;
b. empower victims to determine their future;
c. provide moral support for victims in facing the legal processes they undergo; and
d. make victims feel accepted in the community environment.
(2) Counseling services are conducted by social rehabilitation officers who are gender responsive, consisting of:
a. counselors who have obtained certificates;
b. psychologists; and
c. psychiatrists.
(3) Counseling services are conducted in places that guarantee a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from victims.
(4) Stages of counseling services include:
a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case discussion; and
d. site visits.

Article 7
Women and children from acts of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality of their identity;
f. the right to restitution;
g. the right .....;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right of children to education;
